<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#oscan-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oscan language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oscan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oscan was an Italic language spoken in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by the Samnites, Campanians, Lucanians and related peoples in the second half of the first millennium BCE. It is known from several hundred inscriptions, written in a native alphabet derived from Etruscan as well as in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> and Capua. After the Social War it gave way to Latin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oscan-language.jpg" alt="Oscan language" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Félix Potuit · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
